  "vulnerabilities": [
    {
      "type": "Authorization bypass via unvalidated ETH `value` in timelock scheduling",
      "severity": "medium",
      "vulnerable_function": "_verifySchedule(bytes,bytes4) (used by checkTransaction)",
      "location": "GuardCM._verifySchedule / GuardCM.checkTransaction",
      "explanation": "The guard’s scheduling verification only checks (target, calldata selector, chainId) via `_verifyData(targets[i], callDatas[i], ...)` and does not validate the `value` parameter of `schedule(...)` nor the `values[]` parameter of `scheduleBatch(...)` (both are decoded but ignored). As a result, a Safe transaction can schedule an otherwise-whitelisted call while attaching an arbitrary amount of ETH to be sent by the timelock during execution. If the timelock holds ETH and any whitelisted target can accept ETH (payable function/fallback) and is attacker-controlled (or can be made to forward ETH), the guard’s policy is bypassed and timelock ETH can be drained.",
      "attack_scenario": "1) Identify any (already whitelisted) target contract that is payable and from which the attacker can extract received ETH (e.g., an attacker-controlled whitelisted contract).\n2) From the Safe, call the timelock `schedule(target, value, data, ...)` with `target` set to that whitelisted contract, `data` set to a whitelisted selector (so `_verifyData` passes), and `value` set to the desired ETH amount to steal.\n3) After the timelock delay, execute the operation; the timelock transfers `value` ETH to the target.\n4) Withdraw the received ETH from the target to the attacker’s EOA.",
      "suggested_fix": "Enforce `value == 0` and `values[i] == 0` in `_verifySchedule` (similar to `ProcessBridgedDataArbitrum` enforcing `l2CallValue == 0`), unless explicit ETH transfers are intended. If ETH transfers are intended, extend the authorization scheme to include an allowed value policy (e.g., incorporate `value` into the authorization key or maintain per-target max/allowed values) and validate it during `_verifySchedule`."
    }
  ],
  "overall_explanation": "The core guard logic correctly blocks Safe delegatecalls (when active) and validates timelock `schedule/scheduleBatch` by checking authorized target+selector (and optionally bridged payload verification). However, the guard omits validation of the ETH `value` fields in the timelock scheduling functions, enabling scheduling of authorized calls with arbitrary ETH attached. This can violate intended restrictions and can lead to timelock ETH loss when a whitelisted payable target is attacker-controlled or otherwise allows extracting received ETH."
}
